<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg" data-parallax="0.15"><img src="/assets/img/photos/estimate-laptop.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/estimate-laptop-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/estimate-laptop.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="626" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/our-appraisal-services/">Services</a></li><li aria-current="page">Carrier Estimate Review</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>A Line-by-Line Look at Your Carrier’s Estimate</h1><p class="lede">Before you decide whether to fight a claim, it helps to know exactly where the carrier’s numbers fall short. A focused review tells you whether the gap is real and how large it might be.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="hero-facts"><li><small>Good fit</small><b>Unsure if the payment is fair</b></li><li><small>Output</small><b>Written gap summary</b></li><li><small>Next step</small><b>Supplement, appraisal or accept</b></li></ul></div></section><div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><h2>What we check, line by line</h2>
<p>Carrier estimates are usually written in standard estimating software, and they can look precise. Precise isn’t the same as complete. Our review compares the estimate against the property, your policy and current pricing for your part of Mississippi.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What we look for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Measurements</td><td>Roof squares, pitch, wall and floor areas, linear feet of trim and gutters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scope</td><td>Missing rooms, elevations, slopes or items the damage clearly reaches</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accessories</td><td>Drip edge, starter, ridge vent, flashing, ice-and-water shield where required</td></tr>
<tr><td>Labor charges</td><td>Steep and high charges, tear-out, haul-off, access and protection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pricing</td><td>Unit costs that fit the local market and the date of loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depreciation</td><td>Rates, ages and how holdback can be recovered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overhead and profit</td><td>Whether a general contractor is reasonably needed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code items</td><td>Upgrades required by code, where the policy has ordinance or law coverage</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>Gaps that show up on Mississippi estimates</h2>
<p>Some patterns appear often enough to watch for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Roof slopes left out because only the front elevation was inspected.</li>
<li>Matching ignored on siding, flooring or shingles that can’t be blended.</li>
<li>Pricing from a database update that doesn’t reflect the surge in labor costs after a regional storm.</li>
<li>Interior water damage estimated only where it was visible on the day of inspection.</li>
<li>Contents depreciated at flat rates regardless of condition.</li>
<li>Named-storm deductibles applied to losses that weren’t from a named storm, or the reverse.</li>
</ul>
<p>For more on reading an estimate yourself, see <a href="/claim-advice/line-by-line-estimate-reading/">line-by-line estimate reading</a>.</p>
<h2>Pricing in a Mississippi market</h2>
<p>Unit prices are where many estimates quietly fall short. Estimating databases publish prices by region and month, but a widespread storm changes the market quickly. After a tornado outbreak in the Pine Belt or a hurricane on the Coast, roofers, drywall crews and electricians book up for months and charge more. An estimate priced before the storm, or for a different region, can understate the real cost of hiring someone to do the work.</p>
<p>We compare unit costs against the date of loss and your location, and we note where a contractor’s bid supports a higher number. We also look at minimum charges for small trade visits, which matter when a repair needs an electrician, a plumber and a painter for only a few hours each.</p>
<div class="callout"><b class="callout-t">A review is not a promise</b><p>We’ll tell you what we find, including when the carrier’s estimate is reasonable. Sometimes the honest answer is that the gap isn’t large enough to justify more work.</p></div>
<h2>After the review: three paths</h2>
<h3>Ask for a supplement</h3>
<p>If the gaps are clear — a missed slope, a forgotten room — the carrier may correct them through a supplement once they’re pointed out. Our written summary gives you or your contractor the specifics to make that request.</p>
<h3>Move to appraisal</h3>
<p>If the carrier won’t budge and the gap is meaningful, appraisal may be the next step. The <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/appraisal-payoff-estimator/">appraisal payoff estimator</a> weighs the potential recovery against the cost. If you go forward, we can serve as your appointed appraiser; see <a href="/our-appraisal-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">appraisal demand representation</a>.</p>
<h3>Accept and move on</h3>
<p>If the estimate holds up, you’ll know you were paid fairly and can focus on repairs. That answer has value too: it saves the cost and time of a dispute that wasn’t going to change much.</p>
<h2>What to send</h2>
<p>Send the carrier’s full estimate (not just the summary page), the payment letters, your declarations page and any contractor bids or photos you have. A review works from documents and, where needed, a site visit to verify measurements. If coverage is in dispute, we’ll say so and suggest you talk with a Mississippi attorney about that part.</p>
<h2>Available across Mississippi</h2>
<p>Carrier Estimate Review is available statewide. Inspections are scheduled around your timeline in places such as <a href="/counties-and-cities/biloxi-harrison-county/">Biloxi</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/hattiesburg-forrest-county/">Hattiesburg</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/southaven-desoto-county/">Southaven</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/tupelo-lee-county/">Tupelo</a>.</p>
<h2>Carrier Estimate Review questions</h2><div class="faqs"><details><summary>Do I need a review before demanding appraisal?</summary><p>It isn’t required, but it helps you decide whether appraisal is worth it and gives you a clear picture of where the disagreement lies.</p></details><details><summary>Can you review an estimate for a claim that’s already been paid?</summary><p>Yes, as long as the claim is still open or can be reopened under your policy. Check your policy and confirm any deadlines with a Mississippi attorney.</p></details><details><summary>Will my carrier accept your review?</summary><p>It may or may not. A review is a documented second opinion; if the carrier disagrees, appraisal is one way to resolve the amount.</p></details></div>
